<title>USA: U.S. Jan producer prices fell 0.3 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Jan producer prices fell 0.3 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-02-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly producer price index (1982 equals 100):</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.	Unadjusted</p>
<p> -				 Jan    Dec--(Prev)   Jan97/96</p>
<p> Finished Goods	     -0.3    0.6    0.5     2.5</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	 Unch    0.1    0.1     0.6</p>
<p> -Consumer Foods	    -1.0   -0.1   -0.1     2.5</p>
<p> -Energy Goods		-0.2    3.4    3.1    10.4</p>
<p> Finished Goods Index-X  132.6  132.7  132.7     N/A</p>
<p>X-Data not seasonally adjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.	Unadjusted</p>
<p> Finished Goods		Jan    Dec--(Prev)   Jan97/96</p>
<p> -Gasoline		     2.6    4.0    5.2    19.7</p>
<p> -Heating Oil		 -2.8    5.0    4.9    17.9</p>
<p> -Tobacco Products	   0.1    0.3    0.3     2.7</p>
<p> -Passenger Cars	    -0.4    0.1    0.3     0.2</p>
<p> -Capital Equipment	 Unch    0.1    0.1     0.5</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	  0.2    0.3    0.4     1.0</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	  0.1    0.1    0.1    -0.5</p>
<p> -Manufact Materials	 0.1    0.1    0.2    -0.7</p>
<p> -Construction Materials   0.1   Unch   Unch     2.2</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:	 Seasonally Adj.	Unadjusted</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	  Jan    Dec--(Prev)   Jan97/96</p>
<p> -Intermed.Energy Goods    1.1    2.2    2.5     9.8</p>
<p> Crude Goods		   5.2    4.9    4.2    15.1</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy-X	2.0   Unch    0.2    -3.7</p>
<p> -Food/Feedstuffs	   -1.0   -2.7   -4.3    -2.0</p>
<p> -Nonfood Materials	  9.3   10.5   10.5    28.1</p>
<p> -Energy Materials-Y	12.9   16.5   16.5    50.1</p>
<p> -Petroleum		    4.1    3.9    3.9    32.1</p>
<p>X-Excludes crude petroleum. Y-Includes crude petroleum. Note - Tobacco products and petroleum are not seasonally adjusted.  </p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast January producer prices to rise 0.3 percent. Excluding food and energy prices, they expected a 0.2 percent increase.</p>
<p>The department said the 0.3 percent drop in January PPI was the first monthly decline since October 1994, when the index fell 0.4 percent.</p>
<p>The January report reflects the department's annual recalculation of seasonal adjustment factors.</p>
</text>
